video: realign the enhancement layer by presentation time, not put counts

0013 retires "surplus" enhancement frames whenever the base layer's put count
is ahead of the enhancement layer's. Those counts cannot carry that meaning.
vf_put_count is a monotonic total - vh265_vf_put() does atomic_add(1) per
frame handed back - so the difference is how many more frames the BL decoder
has had RETURNED, not how many stale EL frames are queued. In steady FEL
playback the EL is permanently one to three returns behind, because the frame
the composite is using has not been given back yet, so the condition is true
on almost every vsync and a correctly paired EL frame is destroyed each time.

Measured on a profile 7 FEL disc (Kodi 22, this stack, 2026-09-24): the gap
oscillates 1-3 and never converges, 31 firings retired 105 EL frames in 92
seconds of playback, and the title then stalled outright - shared HEVC core no
longer draining, CAMLCodec input buffer at 95.6%, frame count frozen, every
frame dropped at the video layer. Playback did not recover.

Presentation time is the right discriminator and is already on the vframes.
The BL and EL of one picture carry the same time, so:

  - an EL stamped BEFORE the BL about to be toggled is one the base layer has
    already passed and can never be paired again. That is the single unpaired
    frame 0013 was written for, and it is retired here.
  - an EL stamped AT the BL's time is its pair and must survive - which is
    exactly what the put-count test could not express.

Retirement is capped per base frame: a realignment is a fault, not a steady
state, and draining the queue to chase one would strip the EL as before.
With no usable timestamp on either side the function does nothing, which is
the tree's behaviour before 0013 existed.

dvel_realign=0 disables it on a running box, so the two behaviours can be
compared without a rebuild.

--- a/drivers/media/video_sink/video.c
+++ b/drivers/media/video_sink/video.c
@@ -939,79 +939,88 @@
 	return ret;
 }
 
-/* Re-pair the enhancement layer with the base layer before toggling.
+/* Retire the enhancement frames the base layer has already passed.
