- * for each BL, with no pts comparison (FLAG_CHECK_ES_PTS is not in
- * dolby_vision_flags by default). So a single BL that goes through unpaired -
- * measured once per disc, at the menu->feature segment transition - leaves the
- * EL one frame behind for the REST of the title: every later composite is built
- * from the wrong enhancement layer, and nothing on this chain corrects it.
+ * amdv_parse_metadata_v2_stb() pairs each base-layer frame with whatever sits
+ * at the head of the dvel queue and never compares presentation times -
+ * FLAG_CHECK_ES_PTS is not in dolby_vision_flags by default, so the check its
+ * code appears to make is compiled in but never taken. One base frame that
+ * goes through unpaired therefore leaves the enhancement layer a frame behind
+ * for the rest of the title: every later composite is built from the wrong
+ * enhancement frame, and nothing on this chain puts it back. Measured on
+ * Avatar: Fire and Ash, once in five hours, at the menu->feature transition.
  *
- * The decoders' own put counts say how far apart the layers are, so the fix is
- * arithmetic rather than timing: drop the surplus. No held vsyncs, no dropped BL
- * frames, no risk of starving the EL decoder - the failure mode of an earlier
- * attempt that tried to WAIT for the EL instead, which additionally could never
- * fire because vf_is_fel() requires a src_fmt this decoder never populates.
+ * The question is which enhancement frames are stale, and the decoders' put
+ * counts cannot answer it. vf_put_count is a MONOTONIC TOTAL - vh265_vf_put()
+ * does atomic_add(1) per frame returned - so "bl_cnt - el_cnt" is the running
+ * difference in frames RETURNED, not the number of surplus frames queued. In
+ * steady FEL playback the enhancement layer is always one to three returns
+ * behind, because its frame is still held by the composite that is using it.
+ * Reading that as surplus retires a correctly paired frame on every vsync:
+ * measured on a profile 7 FEL disc, 105 enhancement frames destroyed in 92
+ * seconds, ending in the shared HEVC core stalling with its input buffer 95%
+ * full and playback frozen.
  *
- * Note the format test accepts VFRAME_SIGNAL_FMT_INVALID. That is deliberate and
- * is what the video_receiver.c original does: the legacy h265 decoder driving
- * this chain never calls update_vframe_src_fmt(), so get_vframe_src_fmt()
- * returns INVALID for every frame here.
+ * Presentation time answers it exactly. The base and enhancement frame of one
+ * picture carry the same time, so an enhancement frame stamped BEFORE the base
+ * frame about to be toggled is one the base layer has already gone past and
+ * can never be paired again - the fault this exists for - while one stamped at
+ * the base frame's own time is its pair and must survive. When either side has
+ * no usable timestamp this does nothing at all, which is the tree's behaviour
+ * before any of this existed.
+ *
+ * dvel_realign=0 turns it off on a running box.
+ */
+static bool dvel_realign = true;
+module_param(dvel_realign, bool, 0664);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(dvel_realign, "\n retire stale dv enhancement frames before a bl toggle\n");
+
+/* At most this many enhancement frames may be retired for one base frame.
+ * A realignment is a fault, not a steady state: needing more than a couple
+ * means the assumption above is wrong, and draining the queue to chase it
+ * would strip the enhancement layer exactly as the put-count version did.
  */
+#define DVEL_REALIGN_MAX_DROP	4
+/* Slack on the comparison, to cover rounding on the way in only. */
+#define DVEL_PTS_SLACK_US	1000
+
 static void dvel_realign_to_bl(struct vframe_s *vf)
 {
-	struct provider_aux_req_s req;
+	struct vframe_s *el;
 	enum vframe_signal_fmt_e fmt;
-	u32 bl_cnt;
-	int i;
+	int dropped = 0;
 
-	if (!vf || !is_amdv_enable())
